  "summary": "After declining to renew its Flock Safety contract over immigration data-sharing concerns, LAPD is moving forward with a pilot program to attach hundreds of Axon license plate readers to streetlights, approved by the City Council. The article situates the shift within a broader national backlash against Flock, Axon's growing LAPD business (including a $235M body-camera/Taser deal), and new LAPD policy restrictions on data retention and sharing.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "A pilot program that would attach hundreds of license plate readers, provided by Axon Enterprise, to streetlights around L.A. is expected to launch soon.",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "According to the crowdsourced database DeFlock, about 100 cities have deactivated their Flock cameras, rejected new deals or canceled existing ones",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "Under the department's new license plate reader policy, officials said the LAPD will have complete ownership of the data and will be prohibited from sharing it with certain outside agencies without approval from senior leadership.",
      "paragraph_idx": 25
    },
    {
      "quote": "A report last month by the LAPD inspector general's office found numerous \"limitations\" in the department's existing agreements with its three plate reader vendors \u2014 Flock, Axon and Motorola",
      "paragraph_idx": 24
    }
  ],
